// `staff` — read staff notation, and produce a `std::notation::staff` document.
//
// A region of this adapter is a page of staff notation written in text. It
// expands to one ordinary expression — a call to the staff package's
// `Document` constructor — which is then resolved, checked, and evaluated
// exactly as if the composer had written it out. The adapter reads; the
// package decides. Nothing here computes a sounding pitch, a performed
// duration, an exact tuplet span, or a grace note's timing: those are
// `realize`'s and a performance profile's answers, and an adapter that
// pre-computed them would be deciding at read time what the package defers.
//
// # The spelling
//
//     syntax staff {
//         instrument "bb_clarinet"
//         transposing M2
//         clef treble
//         key d major
//         time (4, 4)
//         spelling shortest_readable
//
//         pickup (1, 4) { g4/4 }
//
//         repeat (2) {
//             bar (4, 4) { c5/4 d5/4. e5/8 f5/4 ~ }
//             bar (4, 4) { f5/8 rest/8 [g4 b4 d5]/4 c5(3/8) rest/8 }
//
//             ending (1) {
//                 bar (4, 4) { slur { tuplet (3, 2) { a4/8 b4/8 c5/8 } d5/4 } grace [e5] f5/2 }
//             }
//
//             ending (2) {
//                 meter (3, 4)
//                 bar (3, 4) { g5/4 a5/4 b5/4 }
//             }
//         }
//     }
//
// One rule covers every form in it: a **word**, then its **parentheses** if it
// takes numbers, then its **braces** if it holds notation. `bar (4, 4) { … }`,
// `tuplet (3, 2) { … }` and `repeat (2) { … }` are the same shape, and
// `slur { … }` is that shape with the numbers left off. A chord and a grace
// hold pitches rather than notation, so they take brackets instead of braces:
// `[g4 b4 d5]/4` and `grace [e5]`.
//
// # How it is read
//
// `recurse_syntax` enters a node before its children and hands each child the
// context the parent chose for it, so this adapter is one traversal: the place
// a numeral was written in arrives *with* the numeral rather than being
// guessed from a tree read a second time. Three places is the whole of the
// inherited context — a body, a form's parentheses, a chord's brackets — and
// they decide the one question a token cannot answer about itself.
//
// A body is read **from its end**, because the data this adapter emits is
// right-nested: what a tie reaches is already in hand when the tie is met.
// Reading backwards is also why so much of the state below is an argument
// waiting for its word: `bar (4, 4) { … }` arrives as its braces, then its
// parentheses, then the word that says what they were. That is a fact about
// this notation and not about the traversal, and a hand-written descent would
// hold the same things.
//
// # Why it is not the layout a musician would draw
//
// The compiler hands an adapter the fixed lexer's tokens grouped by matched
// delimiters and nothing else. There are no lines: `Whitespace` is one
// undifferentiated trivia kind, and a newline is not a token an adapter can
// see. So a spelling that meant one thing per line could not be read at all,
// and the forms are delimited instead. That is the one place this notation
// departs from what the trial note drew, and it is the compiler's shape
// showing through rather than a preference.
//
// `time (4, 4)` is two numbers rather than `4/4` for the same kind of reason:
// the lexer keeps `4/4` whole as one rational token, and a rational is
// reduced, so `4/4` and `1/1` arrive identical. A time signature is a count of
// a unit and not a quotient, so the two numbers are written as two.
//
// # `time` opens a staff and `meter` changes one
//
// A page states `time (4, 4)` at its head, and a change of meter part-way
// through states `meter (3, 4)` where it happens. They are two words because
// there are no lines: a word written above the music and the same word written
// among it arrive here identically, so a single spelling would leave the reader
// guessing which of the two the composer meant — and guessing wrong turns a
// change of meter into a second opening statement, or the reverse. Two words
// is one more thing to learn and nothing left to infer.
//
// # Every bar states what it measures
//
// `bar (4, 4) { … }` says its own meter, and its contents are checked against
// it. Nothing is inherited: the same reason a note states its own octave and
// its own written value is the reason a bar states its own length. A reader
// looking at one bar can say whether it is full without looking anywhere else,
// and so can this adapter — which is what lets the check land on the bar the
// composer wrote rather than on the page.
//
// This is a decision about reading and no longer one about the compiler.
// A meter arriving from an enclosing form is writable now — that is exactly
// what an inherited context is for — and the notation still says it twice,
// because a bar that took its length from the page could not be read alone.
// The inherited context is spent on something smaller and more local instead:
// which of three places a numeral was written in.
//
// `pickup (1, 4) { … }` is a bar of a stated length that is not the meter, and
// it produces a `Bar` like any other: the package has no pickup, because a
// pickup is a bar with fewer beats in it.
//
// # What this adapter wanted and did not get
//
// - **A number `expand` computed still cannot be written back as a numeral.**
//   Prompt 127dcfah gave the language `nat_literal` and four other spellings,
//   and the printer below uses them freely — but they build a `Text`, and what
//   `expand` has to emit is a `Syntax`. So every number in the *reading*
//   direction is still a token the composer wrote, echoed, which is why dots
//   are counted through a four-valued `Dots` rather than a `Nat` and why four
//   dots are refused. Whether `expand` should be rewritten to build its output
//   through the spellings the printer uses is prompt 127dd's to weigh; it is a
//   change to how a working reader is written, not a hole in what it can read.
// - **A list cannot be built.** A group's children arrive as a `List` and
//   there is no way to add one to it, or to join two. Reading them into
//   right-nested data no longer needs a closure — prompt 127dcfaa gave `list`
//   a fold that runs from its end, which is what a body is read with — but a
//   *list* the adapter has to emit is still built by mapping the children it
//   was handed, one written pitch per child. So a group written inside `[ … ]`
//   has nowhere to put the several pitches it might hold, and is not a pitch.
//   Inside `( … )` there is no such limit: the numbers are threaded rather
//   than listed, so a group written there is read exactly like the numerals
//   around it.
// - **A tie's continuation is checked by spelling, not by pitch.** There is no
//   operation reading a pitch out of a token, so `f5` tied to `f#5` is caught
//   by comparing the two spellings the composer wrote. That is the check §2.5
//   of the trial note asks for, and it is also all this adapter can see.
// - **A printed page is a new page, and says so.** `print` below writes a
//   `StaffDocument` back out as notation, so a region is no longer reflowed by
//   the compiler's own rules. What printing does not do is carry anything
//   across: a printed page holds no comment, no blank line and no origin from
//   the page a value was read from, because it was written from the value and
//   the value holds none of those. Preserving them is `edit`'s job, which is
//   why the two are separate operations rather than one.
// - **A refusal still does not quote the numbers it computed, and could now.**
//   "This bar does not hold what it says it measures" points at the bar and
//   stops there rather than saying *by how much*. That was a limit until
//   prompt 127dcfah: a refusal is a `Text`, `ratio_literal` writes one out of
//   an exact rational, and `text_join` puts the two together — the same three
//   operations the printer below is written with. So this is now a sentence
//   this adapter has not written rather than one it cannot, and rewriting the
//   refusals to say how far a bar is out is prompt 127dd's to weigh alongside
//   the numeral question above. The place was always the half that mattered:
//   the package could find the same fault and could not point anywhere.

library {

    // The highest of the three levels, and a promise the compiler checks where
    // this module is imported: this adapter reads a region, serves one
    // structured command against one, and writes a whole page out of a value.
    let level = "generative";

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- reading

    // Why a region was refused, and the node the refusal is about.
    //
    // A refusal carries a node rather than a place: the compiler reads the
    // range off the node, and an adapter has no operation for reading one.
    data Refusal {
        NoRefusal,
        Refused(node: Syntax<TokenTree>, why: Text),
    }

    // The first refusal of two, in the order they were written.
    fn first_refusal(found: Refusal, later: Refusal) -> Refusal {
        match found {
            Refused(node, why) -> Refused(node, why),
            NoRefusal -> later,
        }
    }

    // What sounds first in a stretch of notation, by the spelling the composer
    // wrote for it.
    //
    // A tie reaches whatever sounds next, and "next" is usually in the next
    // bar. Reading from the end means the answer is already in hand when the
    // tie is met.
    data Sound {
        Silent,
        Sounds(spelling: Text, node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // A tie left hanging at the end of a stretch: it continues into whatever
    // follows the stretch, which the stretch itself cannot see.
    data Hanging {
        Closed,
        Hangs(spelling: Text, node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // One header field, once it has been read.
    data Given {
        Absent,
        Stated(node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // The six things a staff says about itself before any notation.
    data Head {
        Head(
            instrument: Given,
            shift: Given,
            written_clef: Given,
            tonality: Given,
            beats: Given,
            spelled: Given,
        ),
    }

    // What one stretch of notation reads to.
    data Reading {
        Reading(
            items: Syntax<TokenTree>,
            span: Ratio,
            opens: Sound,
            hangs: Hanging,
            head: Head,
            refusal: Refusal,
        ),
    }

    // How many dots a written value carries.
    //
    // Four values and not a `Nat`, because the count has to be emitted as a
    // numeral and this adapter can only emit numerals it can spell.
    data Dots {
        NoDots,
        OneDot,
        TwoDots,
        ThreeDots,
    }

    // One more dot, or nothing further.
    fn one_more_dot(sofar: Dots) -> Option<Dots> {
        match sofar {
            NoDots -> Some(OneDot),
            OneDot -> Some(TwoDots),
            TwoDots -> Some(ThreeDots),
            ThreeDots -> None,
        }
    }

    // What the dots multiply the undotted value by: each dot adds half of what
    // the dot before it added.
    fn dotted_factor(dots: Dots) -> Ratio {
        match dots {
            NoDots -> 1,
            OneDot -> 3/2,
            TwoDots -> 7/4,
            ThreeDots -> 15/8,
        }
    }

    // The written value a note or rest states, waiting for what it belongs to.
    data Length {
        NoLength,
        Divided(numeral: Syntax<TokenTree>, dots: Dots, span: Ratio),
        Spanned(numeral: Syntax<TokenTree>, span: Ratio),
    }

    // Whether what is being read is tied on to whatever sounds next.
    data Tying {
        Untied,
        Tied(node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // Up to two numbers a form stated in its parentheses.
    data Numbers {
        NoNumbers,
        OneNumber(first: Syntax<TokenTree>, value: Ratio),
        TwoNumbers(
            first: Syntax<TokenTree>,
            value: Ratio,
            second: Syntax<TokenTree>,
            other: Ratio,
        ),
    }

    // A braces body a form has taken but not yet used.
    data Taken {
        NoBody,
        Took(spot: NodePath, read: Reading),
    }

    // The pitches of a chord or a grace group, waiting for what holds them.
    data Voiced {
        NoVoices,
        Voices(node: Syntax<TokenTree>, spelling: Text, listed: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // One word or literal, waiting for the word that says what it was.
    data Said {
        Said(spot: NodePath, kind: TokenKind, spelling: Text),
    }

    // Up to two of them: `key d major` is the only form that takes two.
    data Words {
        NoWords,
        OneWord(only: Said),
        TwoWords(first: Said, second: Said),
    }

    // The one word a form took, if it took exactly one.
    fn only_word(words: Words) -> Option<Said> {
        match words {
            NoWords -> None,
            OneWord(only) -> Some(only),
            TwoWords(first, second) -> None,
        }
    }

    // What has been read since the last word, reading from the end.
    //
    // Every slot but the first is an argument waiting for the word that claims
    // it, and every one of them is what reading backwards costs rather than
    // what the traversal costs: `bar` arrives after its braces and its
    // parentheses, `c5` after the value written on it, and `clef` after the
    // word it names. A descent written by hand would hold the same seven.
    data Pending {
        Pending(
            read: Reading,
            length: Length,
            dots: Dots,
            tying: Tying,
            numbers: Numbers,
            taken: Taken,
            voiced: Voiced,
            words: Words,
        ),
    }

    // Where the reader is, which is the one thing a token cannot say about
    // itself.
    //
    // `4` in a body is a written value, `4` between a form's parentheses is one
    // of the numbers that form states, and `g4` between a chord's brackets is
    // one of its pitches. The enclosing group knows which, and hands it down.
    data Place {
        InBody,
        InParens,
        InBrackets,
    }

    // The reader, as the recursor carries it.
    //
    // `Unopened` is what the traversal starts with: a region's own braces have
    // not been entered, so there is no path yet to build an empty sequence at
    // and nothing read to put anywhere. Every branch answers it with itself,
    // and the region's braces replace it — which is also how a region that is
    // not written between braces is caught.
    data State {
        Unopened,
        Opened(place: Place, pending: Pending),
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------- emitting

    // Nothing said yet.
    let no_head = Head(Absent, Absent, Absent, Absent, Absent, Absent);

    // A name, as an expression.
    fn named(here: NodePath, role: Nat, spelling: Text) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, role, 1), spelling)
    }

    // `name(one)`, `name(one, two)`, and so on. A call is a name beside its
    // parenthesised arguments, which is what the ordinary parser reads back.
    fn call1(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [one]),
        ])
    }

    fn call2(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        two: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                one,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 3), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                two,
            ]),
        ])
    }

    fn call3(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        two: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        three: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                one,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 3), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                two,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 4), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                three,
            ]),
        ])
    }

    fn call4(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        two: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        three: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        four: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                one,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 3), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                two,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 4), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                three,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 5), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                four,
            ]),
        ])
    }

    fn call5(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        two: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        three: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        four: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        five: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                one,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 3), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                two,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 4), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                three,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 5), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                four,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 6), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                five,
            ]),
        ])
    }

    fn call7(
        here: NodePath,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        one: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        two: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        three: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        four: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        five: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        six: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        seven: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 0), Delimiter.Layout, [
            named(here, role, spelling),
            syntax_group(syntax_built(here, role, 2), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                one,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 3), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                two,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 4), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                three,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 5), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                four,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 6), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                five,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 7), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                six,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(here, role, 8), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                seven,
            ]),
        ])
    }

    // The end of a written sequence.
    fn no_items(here: NodePath) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 31, 0), "NoItems")
    }

    // Nothing read at all.
    fn nothing_read(here: NodePath) -> Reading {
        Reading(no_items(here), 0, Silent, Closed, no_head, NoRefusal)
    }

    // A reader that has read nothing, ready to be threaded through children.
    fn nothing_pending(here: NodePath) -> Pending {
        Pending(
            nothing_read(here),
            NoLength,
            NoDots,
            Untied,
            NoNumbers,
            NoBody,
            NoVoices,
            NoWords,
        )
    }

    // The anchor of the node at `here`, or a zero where the compiler has no
    // range. An anchor is a number whose meaning is the compiler's table: this
    // adapter hands one over and can read nothing from it.
    fn anchored(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, here: NodePath) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        option_fold(
            syntax_token(syntax_built(here, 32, 0), TokenKind.Integer, "0"),
            fn (node: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Syntax<TokenTree> { node },
            syntax_anchor(region, here, syntax_built(here, 33, 0)),
        )
    }

    // `Tie`, as the package spells it.
    fn tie_of(here: NodePath, tying: Tying) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_identifier(
            syntax_built(here, 34, 0),
            match tying {
                Untied -> "Untied",
                Tied(node) -> "TiedOn",
            },
        )
    }

    // `WrittenDuration`, as the package spells it.
    fn written_duration(here: NodePath, length: Length) -> Option<Syntax<TokenTree>> {
        match length {
            NoLength -> None,
            Divided(numeral, dots, span) -> Some(call2(
                here,
                35,
                "NoteValue",
                numeral,
                syntax_token(
                    syntax_built(here, 30, 0),
                    TokenKind.Integer,
                    match dots {
                        NoDots -> "0",
                        OneDot -> "1",
                        TwoDots -> "2",
                        ThreeDots -> "3",
                    },
                ),
            )),
            Spanned(numeral, span) -> Some(call1(here, 36, "ExactSpan", numeral)),
        }
    }

    // The exact span a stated written value covers.
    fn stated_span(length: Length) -> Option<Ratio> {
        match length {
            NoLength -> None,
            Divided(numeral, dots, span) -> Some(span),
            Spanned(numeral, span) -> Some(span),
        }
    }

    // `Beats(count, unit)`, from the two numbers a form stated.
    fn beats_of(here: NodePath, numbers: Numbers) -> Option<Syntax<TokenTree>> {
        match numbers {
            NoNumbers -> None,
            OneNumber(first, value) -> None,
            TwoNumbers(first, value, second, other) -> Some(call2(
                here,
                37,
                "Beats",
                first,
                second,
            )),
        }
    }

    // What a stated meter measures, in whole notes.
    fn beats_span(numbers: Numbers) -> Option<Ratio> {
        match numbers {
            NoNumbers -> None,
            OneNumber(first, value) -> None,
            TwoNumbers(first, count, second, unit) -> Some(ratio_div(count, unit)),
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- pieces

    // The node the region holds at a path, for a refusal to point at. The
    // region itself stands in where there is nothing there, which cannot
    // happen for a path the traversal handed out.
    fn node_at(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, spot: NodePath) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        option_fold(
            region,
            fn (node: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Syntax<TokenTree> { node },
            syntax_at(region, spot),
        )
    }

    // What one child of a chord's brackets turned out to be.
    //
    // A child read at `InBrackets` records itself and nothing else, so the
    // bracket list is built by mapping the children the group was handed. That
    // is the one place this adapter still reads a child as a *value* rather
    // than as a step, and the reason is the list it has to emit rather than
    // the traversal.
    fn said_of(one: State) -> Option<Said> {
        match one {
            Unopened -> None,
            Opened(place, pending) -> match pending {
                Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> only_word(
                    words,
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // Whether a child is a written pitch.
    fn is_pitch(one: State) -> Bool {
        option_fold(
            false,
            fn (said: Said) -> Bool {
                match said {
                    Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> token_kind_equal(
                        kind,
                        TokenKind.PitchLiteral,
                    ),
                }
            },
            said_of(one),
        )
    }

    // One pitch of a chord, written back with the comma that separates it from
    // the next. A layout group is transparent to the parser, so a list of them
    // reads as the list literal it spells.
    fn voice_written(here: NodePath, one: State) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        option_fold(
            syntax_token(syntax_built(here, 44, 0), TokenKind.Error, ""),
            fn (said: Said) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
                match said {
                    Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> syntax_group(
                        syntax_built(spot, 42, 0),
                        Delimiter.Layout,
                        [
                            syntax_token(
                                syntax_built(spot, 41, 0),
                                TokenKind.PitchLiteral,
                                spelling,
                            ),
                            syntax_token(syntax_built(spot, 43, 0), TokenKind.Comma, ","),
                        ],
                    ),
                }
            },
            said_of(one),
        )
    }

    // The spelling of the first pitch among some children, if there is one.
    fn first_voice(parts: List<State>) -> Option<Text> {
        list_fold_from_start(
            None,
            fn (one: State, sofar: Option<Text>) -> Option<Text> {
                match sofar {
                    Some(found) -> Some(found),
                    None -> option_fold(
                        None,
                        fn (said: Said) -> Option<Text> {
                            match said {
                                Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> if token_kind_equal(
                                    kind,
                                    TokenKind.PitchLiteral,
                                ) {
                                    Some(spelling)
                                } else { None },
                            }
                        },
                        said_of(one),
                    ),
                }
            },
            parts,
        )
    }

    // The pitches of `[ … ]`, as the list expression the package's `Chord` and
    // `Grace` take, together with the first of them for a tie to meet.
    fn voiced_of(here: NodePath, parts: List<State>) -> Voiced {
        option_fold(
            NoVoices,
            fn (spelling: Text) -> Voiced {
                Voices(
                    syntax_group(
                        syntax_built(here, 47, 0),
                        Delimiter.Brackets,
                        map(
                            fn (one: State) -> Syntax<TokenTree> { voice_written(here, one) },
                            filter(is_pitch, parts),
                        ),
                    ),
                    spelling,
                    syntax_group(
                        syntax_built(here, 48, 0),
                        Delimiter.Brackets,
                        map(
                            fn (one: State) -> Syntax<TokenTree> { voice_written(here, one) },
                            filter(is_pitch, parts),
                        ),
                    ),
                )
            },
            first_voice(parts),
        )
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- placing

    // What a form leaves behind: everything it took is used up, what follows
    // it is unchanged, and the sequence has grown by one item.
    fn after_item(
        later: Pending,
        items: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        added: Ratio,
        opens: Sound,
        hangs: Hanging,
        trouble: Refusal,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match read {
                Reading(was, span, was_open, was_hanging, head, refusal) -> later with {
                    read = Reading(
                        items,
                        ratio_add(span, added),
                        opens,
                        hangs,
                        head,
                        first_refusal(trouble, refusal),
                    ),
                    length = NoLength,
                    dots = NoDots,
                    tying = Untied,
                    numbers = NoNumbers,
                    taken = NoBody,
                    voiced = NoVoices,
                    words = NoWords
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // A tie left open by what is being placed, met against what follows it.
    //
    // The tie is checked by spelling because a spelling is what the composer
    // wrote and all this adapter can see: `f5` tied to `f#5` is two different
    // texts, which is exactly the mistake worth catching.
    fn tie_met(hangs: Hanging, next: Sound, beyond: Hanging) -> Result<Hanging, Refusal> {
        match hangs {
            Closed -> Ok(beyond),
            Hangs(spelling, node) -> match next {
                Silent -> Ok(Hangs(spelling, node)),
                Sounds(other, at_node) -> if text_equal(spelling, other) {
                    Ok(beyond)
                } else {
                    Err(Refused(at_node, "this staff ties a note to a differently spelled one"))
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // Everything a placed item needs to know about what follows it.
    fn following(later: Pending) -> Reading {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> read,
        }
    }

    // One sounding item — a note, a rest, a chord, a grace group, a meter
    // change — placed in front of what follows it.
    fn sounding(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        event: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        added: Ratio,
        opens: Sound,
        hangs: Hanging,
    ) -> Pending {
        match following(later) {
            Reading(items, span, next, beyond, head, refusal) -> match tie_met(
                hangs,
                next,
                beyond,
            ) {
                Ok(reached) -> after_item(
                    later,
                    call3(here, 1, "Sounded", anchored(region, here), event, items),
                    added,
                    match opens {
                        Silent -> next,
                        Sounds(spelling, node) -> Sounds(spelling, node),
                    },
                    reached,
                    NoRefusal,
                ),
                Err(why) -> after_item(later, items, added, next, beyond, why),
            },
        }
    }

    // A nested form — a bar, a slur, a tuplet, a repeat, an ending — placed in
    // front of what follows it, carrying its body's own reading with it.
    //
    // What went wrong inside is reported before what went wrong around it: a
    // bar whose notation the reader could not read does not measure what it
    // says either, and that second sentence is a consequence of the first
    // rather than a second fault.
    fn nesting(
        here: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        built: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        body: Reading,
        added: Ratio,
        trouble: Refusal,
    ) -> Pending {
        match body {
            Reading(inner, inner_span, inner_opens, inner_hangs, inner_head, inner_refusal) -> match following(
                later,
            ) {
                Reading(items, span, next, beyond, head, refusal) -> match tie_met(
                    inner_hangs,
                    next,
                    beyond,
                ) {
                    Ok(reached) -> after_item(
                        later,
                        built,
                        added,
                        match inner_opens {
                            Silent -> next,
                            Sounds(spelling, node) -> Sounds(spelling, node),
                        },
                        reached,
                        first_refusal(inner_refusal, trouble),
                    ),
                    Err(why) -> after_item(
                        later,
                        items,
                        added,
                        next,
                        beyond,
                        first_refusal(trouble, first_refusal(inner_refusal, why)),
                    ),
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // A refusal that leaves what is being read where it is.
    //
    // `refusing` ends the item it is reading, which is right when the item is
    // the fault. A fault found while a written value is still in hand is not:
    // clearing it would make the note that value belongs to look valueless,
    // and the reader would go on to say so — a second sentence about a note
    // that is not what went wrong.
    fn faulting(later: Pending, node: Syntax<TokenTree>, why: Text) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match read {
                Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> later with {
                    read = Reading(
                        items,
                        span,
                        opens,
                        hangs,
                        head,
                        first_refusal(Refused(node, why), refusal),
                    )
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // A refusal, with nothing placed.
    fn refusing(later: Pending, node: Syntax<TokenTree>, why: Text) -> Pending {
        match following(later) {
            Reading(items, span, next, beyond, head, refusal) -> after_item(
                later,
                items,
                0,
                next,
                beyond,
                Refused(node, why),
            ),
        }
    }

    // ----------------------------------------------------------- what is held

    // The written value the next note takes, or the refusal that says it was
    // stated twice.
    //
    // A value is spent the moment the note it belongs to is read, so a value
    // arriving while one is already held belongs to no note that has been
    // read: `c5/4/8` states two, and neither of them is more the note's than
    // the other.
    fn holding_length(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        length: Length,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(r, was, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match was {
                // The left `length` is the field; the right is this
                // function's parameter. A right-hand side reads the scope
                // around the update, never the fields of its subject.
                NoLength -> later with {
                    length = length,
                    dots = NoDots
                },
                Divided(numeral, held_dots, span) -> faulting(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this note states two written values",
                ),
                Spanned(numeral, span) -> faulting(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this note states two written values",
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // One more word waiting for the word that says what it was. A third is one
    // more than any form in this notation takes.
    fn holding_word(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, later: Pending, said: Said) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(r, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match words {
                NoWords -> later with {
                    words = OneWord(said)
                },
                OneWord(only) -> later with {
                    words = TwoWords(said, only)
                },
                TwoWords(first, second) -> match said {
                    Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> refusing(
                        later,
                        node_at(region, spot),
                        "this staff does not know this word",
                    ),
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // The head this reading states, with one more field filled in, or the
    // refusal that says it was stated twice.
    //
    // Five words, and a head holds one of each. A sixth statement of the same
    // word is not a correction: nothing here is written in order, so the
    // reader has no way to say which of the two the composer meant.
    fn stating(
        head: Head,
        which: Text,
        given: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        node: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Result<Head, Refusal> {
        match head {
            Head(instrument, shift, written_clef, tonality, beats, spelled) -> match which {
                "instrument" -> match instrument {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        instrument = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
                "transposing" -> match shift {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        shift = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
                "clef" -> match written_clef {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        written_clef = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
                "key" -> match tonality {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        tonality = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
                "time" -> match beats {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        beats = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
                _ -> match spelled {
                    Absent -> Ok(head with {
                        spelled = Stated(given)
                    }),
                    Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // A header field, read and put away. Nothing is placed: a header is what
    // the staff is, not something on it.
    fn heading(
        later: Pending,
        which: Text,
        given: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        node: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match read {
                Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> match stating(
                    head,
                    which,
                    given,
                    node,
                ) {
                    Ok(stated) -> later with {
                        read = Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, stated, refusal),
                        length = NoLength,
                        dots = NoDots,
                        tying = Untied,
                        numbers = NoNumbers,
                        taken = NoBody,
                        voiced = NoVoices,
                        words = NoWords
                    },
                    Err(why) -> later with {
                        read = Reading(
                            items,
                            span,
                            opens,
                            hangs,
                            head,
                            first_refusal(why, refusal),
                        ),
                        length = NoLength,
                        dots = NoDots,
                        tying = Untied,
                        numbers = NoNumbers,
                        taken = NoBody,
                        voiced = NoVoices,
                        words = NoWords
                    },
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the forms

    // What a clef word names.
    fn clef_named(here: NodePath, spelling: Text) -> Option<Syntax<TokenTree>> {
        if text_equal(spelling, "treble") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 38, 0), "Treble"))
        } else if text_equal(spelling, "bass") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 38, 1), "Bass"))
        } else if text_equal(spelling, "alto") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 38, 2), "Alto"))
        } else if text_equal(spelling, "tenor") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 38, 3), "Tenor"))
        } else { None }
    }

    // What a spelling word names.
    fn spelling_named(here: NodePath, spelling: Text) -> Option<Syntax<TokenTree>> {
        if text_equal(spelling, "shortest_readable") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 39, 0), "ShortestReadable"))
        } else if text_equal(spelling, "exact_values") {
            Some(syntax_identifier(syntax_built(here, 39, 1), "ExactValues"))
        } else { None }
    }

    // `rest/8` — a written silence.
    fn rest_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        length: Length,
    ) -> Pending {
        match written_duration(spot, length) {
            None -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this rest states no written value"),
            Some(value) -> option_fold(
                refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this rest states no written value"),
                fn (span: Ratio) -> Pending {
                    sounding(
                        region,
                        spot,
                        later,
                        call1(spot, 3, "Rest", value),
                        span,
                        Silent,
                        Closed,
                    )
                },
                stated_span(length),
            ),
        }
    }

    // `c5/4`, `d5/4.`, `f5/4 ~`, `c5(3/8)` — one written note.
    fn note_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        spelling: Text,
        length: Length,
        tying: Tying,
    ) -> Pending {
        match written_duration(spot, length) {
            None -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this note states no written value"),
            Some(value) -> option_fold(
                refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this note states no written value"),
                fn (span: Ratio) -> Pending {
                    sounding(
                        region,
                        spot,
                        later,
                        call3(
                            spot,
                            2,
                            "Note",
                            syntax_token(
                                syntax_built(spot, 49, 0),
                                TokenKind.PitchLiteral,
                                spelling,
                            ),
                            value,
                            tie_of(spot, tying),
                        ),
                        span,
                        Sounds(spelling, node_at(region, spot)),
                        match tying {
                            Untied -> Closed,
                            Tied(node) -> Hangs(spelling, node),
                        },
                    )
                },
                stated_span(length),
            ),
        }
    }

    // `[g4 b4 d5]/4` — one written chord.
    fn chord_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        voiced: Voiced,
        length: Length,
        tying: Tying,
    ) -> Pending {
        match voiced {
            NoVoices -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this chord holds no pitches"),
            Voices(node, spelling, listed) -> match written_duration(spot, length) {
                None -> refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this chord states no written value",
                ),
                Some(value) -> option_fold(
                    refusing(
                        later,
                        node_at(region, spot),
                        "this chord states no written value",
                    ),
                    fn (span: Ratio) -> Pending {
                        sounding(
                            region,
                            spot,
                            later,
                            call3(spot, 4, "Chord", listed, value, tie_of(spot, tying)),
                            span,
                            Sounds(spelling, node_at(region, spot)),
                            match tying {
                                Untied -> Closed,
                                Tied(hung) -> Hangs(spelling, hung),
                            },
                        )
                    },
                    stated_span(length),
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // What follows the item being placed.
    fn after_items(later: Pending) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        match following(later) {
            Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> items,
        }
    }

    // `bar (4, 4) { … }` and `pickup (1, 4) { … }` — a measure of stated
    // length, checked against what it holds.
    //
    // This is one of the two diagnostics the trial note asks for, and it lands
    // on the bar the composer wrote because the adapter is the one holding a
    // node. The package could find the same fault and could not point at it.
    fn bar_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        numbers: Numbers,
        taken: Taken,
    ) -> Pending {
        match taken {
            NoBody -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this bar holds no notation"),
            Took(body_at, body) -> option_fold(
                refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this bar does not say what it measures",
                ),
                fn (wanted: Ratio) -> Pending {
                    option_fold(
                        refusing(
                            later,
                            node_at(region, spot),
                            "this bar does not say what it measures",
                        ),
                        fn (beats: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Pending {
                            match body {
                                Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> nesting(
                                    spot,
                                    later,
                                    call4(
                                        spot,
                                        7,
                                        "Bar",
                                        anchored(region, spot),
                                        beats,
                                        items,
                                        after_items(later),
                                    ),
                                    body,
                                    wanted,
                                    if ratio_equal(span, wanted) {
                                        NoRefusal
                                    } else {
                                        Refused(
                                            node_at(region, spot),
                                            "this bar does not hold what it says it measures",
                                        )
                                    },
                                ),
                            }
                        },
                        beats_of(spot, numbers),
                    )
                },
                beats_span(numbers),
            ),
        }
    }

    // `slur { … }` — one phrase mark over what it holds.
    fn slur_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        taken: Taken,
    ) -> Pending {
        match taken {
            NoBody -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this slur holds no notation"),
            Took(body_at, body) -> match body {
                Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> nesting(
                    spot,
                    later,
                    call3(spot, 8, "Slur", anchored(region, spot), items, after_items(later)),
                    body,
                    span,
                    NoRefusal,
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // `tuplet (3, 2) { … }` — three in the time of two, and the bracket is
    // kept rather than resolved: what the notes become is `realize`'s answer.
    fn tuplet_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        numbers: Numbers,
        taken: Taken,
    ) -> Pending {
        match taken {
            NoBody -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this tuplet holds no notation"),
            Took(body_at, body) -> match numbers {
                NoNumbers -> refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this tuplet states no ratio",
                ),
                OneNumber(first, value) -> refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this tuplet states no ratio",
                ),
                TwoNumbers(first, played, second, against) -> match ratio_equal(played, 0/1) {
                    true -> refusing(
                        later,
                        node_at(region, spot),
                        "this tuplet plays no notes in the time of some",
                    ),
                    false -> match body {
                        Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> nesting(
                            spot,
                            later,
                            call5(
                                spot,
                                9,
                                "Tuplet",
                                anchored(region, spot),
                                first,
                                second,
                                items,
                                after_items(later),
                            ),
                            body,
                            ratio_mul(span, ratio_div(against, played)),
                            NoRefusal,
                        ),
                    },
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // `repeat (2) { … }` and `ending (1) { … }`.
    //
    // Written time counts the page and not the performance, so a repeat covers
    // its body once: how many times it sounds is a reading of the score.
    fn repeated_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        role: Nat,
        spelling: Text,
        numbers: Numbers,
        taken: Taken,
        missing: Text,
    ) -> Pending {
        match taken {
            NoBody -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), missing),
            Took(body_at, body) -> match numbers {
                NoNumbers -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), missing),
                OneNumber(first, value) -> match body {
                    Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> nesting(
                        spot,
                        later,
                        call4(
                            spot,
                            role,
                            spelling,
                            anchored(region, spot),
                            first,
                            items,
                            after_items(later),
                        ),
                        body,
                        span,
                        NoRefusal,
                    ),
                },
                TwoNumbers(first, value, second, other) -> refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    missing,
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // `time (4, 4)` — the meter this staff opens in.
    //
    // A different word from the `meter (3, 4)` that changes it, and the region
    // grouper is the reason. An adapter cannot see lines, so it cannot tell a
    // word written above the music from the same word written among it; two
    // words is what that costs. The notation carries the cost well enough — a
    // staff opens in a time signature and changes meter later — and the head
    // reads like the five statements beside it rather than like an item.
    fn time_header(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        numbers: Numbers,
    ) -> Pending {
        option_fold(
            refusing(
                later,
                node_at(region, spot),
                "this staff does not say what time it opens in",
            ),
            fn (beats: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Pending {
                heading(later, "time", beats, node_at(region, spot))
            },
            beats_of(spot, numbers),
        )
    }

    // `meter (3, 4)` — the meter changes here, and the bars after it say so
    // themselves.
    fn meter_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        numbers: Numbers,
    ) -> Pending {
        option_fold(
            refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this meter states no count and unit"),
            fn (beats: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Pending {
                sounding(
                    region,
                    spot,
                    later,
                    call1(spot, 6, "MeterChange", beats),
                    0,
                    Silent,
                    Closed,
                )
            },
            beats_of(spot, numbers),
        )
    }

    // `grace [e5]` — written pitches with no written duration. What a grace
    // takes, and what it takes it from, is a performance profile's choice.
    fn grace_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        voiced: Voiced,
    ) -> Pending {
        match voiced {
            NoVoices -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this grace holds no pitches"),
            Voices(node, spelling, listed) -> sounding(
                region,
                spot,
                later,
                call1(spot, 5, "Grace", listed),
                0,
                Silent,
                Closed,
            ),
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the header

    // Why a header field did not take exactly one word.
    //
    // No word at all is an empty field. Two words means one of them stands
    // between the field and its argument, and nothing in this staff reads a
    // word in that position — so the useful sentence names *that* word rather
    // than reporting the field as empty, which it is not. The reader works
    // from the end, so the field's own argument is the nearer of the two and
    // the stray one is the further.
    fn stray_word(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Pending {
        match words {
            NoWords -> refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this staff field says nothing"),
            OneWord(only) -> refusing(
                later,
                node_at(region, spot),
                "this staff field says nothing",
            ),
            TwoWords(first, second) -> match second {
                Said(stray_at, stray_kind, spelling) -> refusing(
                    later,
                    node_at(region, stray_at),
                    "this staff does not know this word",
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // `instrument "bb_clarinet"` and `transposing M2` — a literal written back
    // as the composer spelled it.
    fn literal_header(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        which: Text,
        kind: TokenKind,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Pending {
        option_fold(
            stray_word(region, spot, later, words),
            fn (said: Said) -> Pending {
                match said {
                    Said(word_at, said_kind, spelling) -> if token_kind_equal(said_kind, kind) {
                        heading(
                            later,
                            which,
                            syntax_token(syntax_built(word_at, 50, 0), kind, spelling),
                            node_at(region, word_at),
                        )
                    } else {
                        refusing(
                            later,
                            node_at(region, word_at),
                            "this staff field was given the wrong kind of word",
                        )
                    },
                }
            },
            only_word(words),
        )
    }

    // `clef treble` and `spelling shortest_readable` — a word this adapter
    // knows, or the refusal that says it does not.
    fn named_header(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        which: Text,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Pending {
        option_fold(
            stray_word(region, spot, later, words),
            fn (said: Said) -> Pending {
                match said {
                    Said(word_at, said_kind, spelling) -> option_fold(
                        refusing(
                            later,
                            node_at(region, word_at),
                            "this staff does not know this word",
                        ),
                        fn (given: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Pending {
                            heading(later, which, given, node_at(region, word_at))
                        },
                        if text_equal(which, "clef") {
                            clef_named(word_at, spelling)
                        } else { spelling_named(word_at, spelling) },
                    ),
                }
            },
            only_word(words),
        )
    }

    // `key d major` — written back as the key expression the composer wrote,
    // parenthesised so that it reads as one argument.
    fn key_header(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        later: Pending,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Pending {
        match words {
            TwoWords(first, second) -> match first {
                Said(tonic_at, tonic_kind, tonic) -> match second {
                    Said(mode_at, mode_kind, mode) -> heading(
                        later,
                        "key",
                        syntax_group(syntax_built(spot, 51, 0), Delimiter.Parentheses, [
                            syntax_token(syntax_built(spot, 51, 1), TokenKind.KeyKw, "key"),
                            syntax_token(
                                syntax_built(tonic_at, 52, 0),
                                TokenKind.Identifier,
                                tonic,
                            ),
                            syntax_token(
                                syntax_built(mode_at, 52, 1),
                                TokenKind.Identifier,
                                mode,
                            ),
                        ]),
                        node_at(region, spot),
                    ),
                },
            },
            NoWords -> refusing(
                later,
                node_at(region, spot),
                "this key names no tonic and no mode",
            ),
            OneWord(only) -> refusing(
                later,
                node_at(region, spot),
                "this key names no tonic and no mode",
            ),
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the reader

    // One word, and what it does with whatever was read since the last one.
    //
    // Every word the staff spelling has, in one table. A word either opens a
    // form — a bar, a slur, a tuplet, a repeat — or states one of the six
    // facts a document carries in its head. Anything else is a word this staff
    // does not know, and is held rather than refused here, because what comes
    // after it is often what says which word it was meant to be.
    fn word_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        spelling: Text,
        kind: TokenKind,
        later: Pending,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match spelling {
                "rest" -> rest_read(region, spot, later, length),
                "bar" -> bar_read(region, spot, later, numbers, taken),
                "pickup" -> bar_read(region, spot, later, numbers, taken),
                "slur" -> slur_read(region, spot, later, taken),
                "tuplet" -> tuplet_read(region, spot, later, numbers, taken),
                "grace" -> grace_read(region, spot, later, voiced),
                "repeat" -> repeated_read(
                    region,
                    spot,
                    later,
                    10,
                    "Repeat",
                    numbers,
                    taken,
                    "this repeat does not say how many times it holds what",
                ),
                "ending" -> repeated_read(
                    region,
                    spot,
                    later,
                    11,
                    "Ending",
                    numbers,
                    taken,
                    "this ending does not say which pass it holds what for",
                ),
                "time" -> time_header(region, spot, later, numbers),
                "meter" -> meter_read(region, spot, later, numbers),
                "instrument" -> literal_header(
                    region,
                    spot,
                    later,
                    "instrument",
                    TokenKind.String,
                    words,
                ),
                "transposing" -> literal_header(
                    region,
                    spot,
                    later,
                    "transposing",
                    TokenKind.IntervalLiteral,
                    words,
                ),
                "clef" -> named_header(region, spot, later, "clef", words),
                "spelling" -> named_header(region, spot, later, "spelling", words),
                "key" -> key_header(region, spot, later, words),
                _ -> holding_word(region, later, Said(spot, kind, spelling)),
            },
        }
    }

    // One token written in a body, by what the lexer called it.
    fn body_token(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        kind: TokenKind,
        spelling: Text,
        later: Pending,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> if token_kind_equal(
                kind,
                TokenKind.Tilde,
            ) {
                later with {
                    tying = Tied(node_at(region, spot))
                }
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Dot) {
                option_fold(
                    refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this value carries too many dots"),
                    fn (more: Dots) -> Pending { later with { dots = more } },
                    one_more_dot(dots),
                )
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Integer) {
                option_fold(
                    refusing(later, node_at(region, spot), "this is not a written value"),
                    fn (value: Ratio) -> Pending {
                        // `c4/0` is the one number that names no value, and the
                        // guard is written rather than caught: dividing by zero
                        // is a refusal the checker makes, and a refused adapter
                        // says nothing about the note the author actually wrote.
                        // The tuplet reader guards its own ratio the same way.
                        match ratio_equal(value, 0/1) {
                            true -> refusing(
                                later,
                                node_at(region, spot),
                                "this is not a written value",
                            ),
                            false -> holding_length(
                                region,
                                spot,
                                later,
                                Divided(
                                    syntax_token(
                                        syntax_built(spot, 53, 0),
                                        TokenKind.Integer,
                                        spelling,
                                    ),
                                    dots,
                                    ratio_div(dotted_factor(dots), value),
                                ),
                            ),
                        }
                    },
                    syntax_number(node_at(region, spot)),
                )
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.PitchLiteral) {
                note_read(region, spot, later, spelling, length, tying)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Slash) {
                later
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Comma) {
                later
            } else { word_read(region, spot, spelling, kind, later) },
        }
    }

    // One more of the up-to-two numbers a form states between its parentheses.
    fn one_more_number(
        spot: NodePath,
        numbers: Numbers,
        spelling: Text,
        value: Ratio,
    ) -> Numbers {
        match numbers {
            NoNumbers -> OneNumber(
                syntax_token(syntax_built(spot, 45, 0), TokenKind.Integer, spelling),
                value,
            ),
            OneNumber(first, already) -> TwoNumbers(
                first,
                already,
                syntax_token(syntax_built(spot, 45, 0), TokenKind.Integer, spelling),
                value,
            ),
            TwoNumbers(first, already, second, other) -> numbers,
        }
    }

    // One token written between a form's parentheses.
    //
    // An integer is one of the numbers the form states, and a rational is the
    // exact span `c5(3/8)` writes. Anything else — the comma, whitespace —
    // states nothing, and the reading is unchanged.
    fn stated_token(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        spot: NodePath,
        kind: TokenKind,
        spelling: Text,
        later: Pending,
    ) -> Pending {
        match later {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> if token_kind_equal(
                kind,
                TokenKind.Integer,
            ) {
                option_fold(
                    later,
                    fn (value: Ratio) -> Pending {
                        later with {
                            numbers = one_more_number(spot, numbers, spelling, value)
                        }
                    },
                    syntax_number(node_at(region, spot)),
                )
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Rational) {
                match length {
                    NoLength -> option_fold(
                        later,
                        fn (value: Ratio) -> Pending {
                            later with {
                                length = Spanned(
                                    syntax_token(
                                        syntax_built(spot, 46, 0),
                                        TokenKind.Rational,
                                        spelling,
                                    ),
                                    value,
                                )
                            }
                        },
                        syntax_number(node_at(region, spot)),
                    ),
                    Divided(numeral, held_dots, span) -> later,
                    Spanned(numeral, span) -> later,
                }
            } else { later },
        }
    }

    // Where a reader stands, with the region's own braces standing for a body.
    fn place_of(state: State) -> Place {
        match state {
            Unopened -> InBody,
            Opened(place, pending) -> place,
        }
    }

    // What a reader has read, with nothing standing for nothing.
    fn pending_of(here: NodePath, state: State) -> Pending {
        match state {
            Unopened -> nothing_pending(here),
            Opened(place, pending) -> pending,
        }
    }

    // A reader standing where it stood, holding what it now holds.
    //
    // With `place_of` and `pending_of` this is the whole of what the three
    // step functions need from `State`: every one of them reads a place, works
    // on a pending, and stands where it stood. Saying that once is why none of
    // them carries an arm for a state that has not been opened.
    fn reopened(state: State, pending: Pending) -> State { Opened(place_of(state), pending) }

    // Anything read and never used is something this staff did not understand.
    fn leftover(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        length: Length,
        numbers: Numbers,
        taken: Taken,
        voiced: Voiced,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Refusal {
        match words {
            OneWord(only) -> match only {
                Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> Refused(
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this staff does not know this word",
                ),
            },
            TwoWords(first, second) -> match first {
                Said(spot, kind, spelling) -> Refused(
                    node_at(region, spot),
                    "this staff does not know this word",
                ),
            },
            NoWords -> match voiced {
                Voices(node, spelling, listed) -> Refused(
                    node,
                    "these pitches are held for nothing",
                ),
                NoVoices -> match taken {
                    Took(spot, read) -> Refused(
                        node_at(region, spot),
                        "this staff does not know what holds this",
                    ),
                    NoBody -> match numbers {
                        OneNumber(first, value) -> Refused(
                            node_at(region, here),
                            "this staff does not know what these numbers are for",
                        ),
                        TwoNumbers(first, value, second, other) -> Refused(
                            node_at(region, here),
                            "this staff does not know what these numbers are for",
                        ),
                        NoNumbers -> match length {
                            Divided(numeral, dots, span) -> Refused(
                                node_at(region, here),
                                "this written value belongs to nothing",
                            ),
                            Spanned(numeral, span) -> Refused(
                                node_at(region, here),
                                "this written value belongs to nothing",
                            ),
                            NoLength -> NoRefusal,
                        },
                    },
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // Everything between one pair of braces, read.
    //
    // The steps are run from the end because the data this adapter emits is
    // right-nested: what a tie reaches is already in hand when the tie is met.
    // Each step carries the reading so far into the child it stands for, which
    // is the whole of what the recursor adds — the children are read once, in
    // the order and under the context this line chooses.
    fn body_reading(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> Reading {
        match pending_of(
            here,
            list_fold_from_end(
                Opened(InBody, nothing_pending(here)),
                fn (kid, later) { run_syntax_step(later, kid) },
                kids,
            ),
        ) {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match read {
                Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> read with {
                    refusal = first_refusal(
                        leftover(region, here, length, numbers, taken, voiced, words),
                        refusal,
                    )
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // Everything between one pair of parentheses, read.
    //
    // Left to right, because the first numeral is the count and the second is
    // the unit, and threaded rather than listed — which is why a group written
    // in here is read like the numerals around it rather than dropped.
    fn stated_inside(here: NodePath, kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>) -> Pending {
        pending_of(
            here,
            list_fold_from_start(
                Opened(InParens, nothing_pending(here)),
                fn (kid, earlier) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
                kids,
            ),
        )
    }

    // Every child of a chord's brackets, read as itself.
    //
    // Mapped rather than threaded: what comes out of here is a `List` this
    // adapter has to hand to `syntax_group`, and a list can only be built by
    // mapping one the compiler already handed it.
    fn voiced_inside(here: NodePath, kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>) -> List<State> {
        map(fn (kid) { run_syntax_step(Opened(InBrackets, nothing_pending(here)), kid) }, kids)
    }

    // A braces body, held for the word that says what holds it.
    fn holding_body(pending: Pending, here: NodePath, body: Reading) -> Pending {
        pending with {
            taken = Took(here, body)
        }
    }

    // A braces body, taken into whatever the reader was already doing.
    fn taking(here: NodePath, state: State, body: Reading) -> State {
        Opened(place_of(state), holding_body(pending_of(here, state), here, body))
    }

    // `( … )` met in a body: either the exact span `c5(3/8)` writes, or the
    // numbers a form states.
    fn parens_pending(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        pending: Pending,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> Pending {
        match stated_inside(here, kids) {
            Pending(read, exact, dots, tying, stated, taken, voiced, words) -> match exact {
                NoLength -> pending with {
                    numbers = stated
                },
                Divided(numeral, held_dots, span) -> pending with {
                    numbers = stated
                },
                Spanned(numeral, span) -> holding_length(region, here, pending, exact),
            },
        }
    }

    // `[ … ]` met in a body: a chord when a written value is already in hand,
    // and otherwise pitches held for the word that takes them.
    fn brackets_pending(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        pending: Pending,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> Pending {
        match pending {
            Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match length {
                NoLength -> pending with {
                    voiced = voiced_of(here, voiced_inside(here, kids))
                },
                Divided(numeral, held_dots, span) -> chord_read(
                    region,
                    here,
                    pending,
                    voiced_of(here, voiced_inside(here, kids)),
                    length,
                    tying,
                ),
                Spanned(numeral, span) -> chord_read(
                    region,
                    here,
                    pending,
                    voiced_of(here, voiced_inside(here, kids)),
                    length,
                    tying,
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // One group met in a body, by what encloses it.
    fn entered(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        delimiter: Delimiter,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter.Braces) {
            taking(here, state, body_reading(region, here, kids))
        } else if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter.Parentheses) {
            reopened(state, parens_pending(region, here, pending_of(here, state), kids))
        } else if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter.Brackets) {
            reopened(state, brackets_pending(region, here, pending_of(here, state), kids))
        } else { state }
    }

    // One group, read under the place it stands in.
    //
    // In a body a group is a form's own punctuation, and which punctuation it
    // is decides what its children mean. Between parentheses there is nothing
    // for a nested group to be but more of the same, so its children are read
    // as the numerals around it are. Between brackets a group is not a pitch,
    // for the reason the list at the top of this file gives.
    fn group_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        delimiter: Delimiter,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        match place_of(state) {
            InBody -> entered(region, state, here, delimiter, kids),
            InParens -> list_fold_from_start(
                state,
                fn (kid, earlier) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
                kids,
            ),
            InBrackets -> state,
        }
    }

    // Whether a token is spacing rather than notation. Trivia says nothing
    // wherever it is written, which is the one rule that holds in all three
    // places.
    fn trivial(kind: TokenKind) -> Bool {
        if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.Whitespace) {
            true
        } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.LineComment) {
            true
        } else { token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind.BlockComment) }
    }

    // The one word a bracketed child amounts to. Each child is read from a
    // blank pending of its own, so there is never an earlier word for this one
    // to stack behind — which is the difference from `holding_word`.
    fn only_said(pending: Pending, said: Said) -> Pending {
        pending with {
            words = OneWord(said)
        }
    }

    // One token, read under the place it stands in.
    fn token_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        kind: TokenKind,
        spelling: Text,
    ) -> State {
        if trivial(kind) {
            state
        } else {
            match place_of(state) {
                InBody -> reopened(
                    state,
                    body_token(region, here, kind, spelling, pending_of(here, state)),
                ),
                InParens -> reopened(
                    state,
                    stated_token(region, here, kind, spelling, pending_of(here, state)),
                ),
                InBrackets -> reopened(
                    state,
                    only_said(pending_of(here, state), Said(here, kind, spelling)),
                ),
            }
        }
    }

    // A node the fixed lexer could not read. It is a fault where notation was
    // expected and nothing at all where numbers or pitches were.
    fn missing_read(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, state: State, here: NodePath) -> State {
        match place_of(state) {
            InBody -> reopened(
                state,
                refusing(
                    pending_of(here, state),
                    node_at(region, here),
                    "this staff cannot read this",
                ),
            ),
            InParens -> state,
            InBrackets -> state,
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- the document

    // A field the staff had to state.
    fn stated_field(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        field: Given,
        missing: Text,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<TokenTree>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        match field {
            Absent -> Err((node_at(region, here), missing)),
            Stated(node) -> Ok(node),
        }
    }

    // The whole staff, as the one expression a region expands to.
    fn document_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        read: Reading,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<TokenTree>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        match read {
            Reading(items, span, opens, hangs, head, refusal) -> match refusal {
                Refused(node, why) -> Err((node, why)),
                NoRefusal -> match hangs {
                    Hangs(spelling, node) -> Err((
                        node,
                        "this staff ties a note to nothing: no note sounds after it"
                    )),
                    Closed -> match head {
                        // Six things the head has to have said, asked in the
                        // order a reader would ask them. Each `?` hands the
                        // first refusal straight back out, so what is left on
                        // the page is the document being built rather than the
                        // six ways it might not be.
                        Head(instrument, shift, written_clef, tonality, beats, spelled) -> Ok(call7(
                            here,
                            19,
                            "Document",
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                instrument,
                                "this staff does not say what instrument it is written for",
                            )?,
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                shift,
                                "this staff does not say how far its written pitch sits from its sounding pitch",
                            )?,
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                written_clef,
                                "this staff does not say what clef it is read in",
                            )?,
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                tonality,
                                "this staff does not say what key it is written in",
                            )?,
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                beats,
                                "this staff does not say what time it opens in",
                            )?,
                            stated_field(
                                region,
                                here,
                                spelled,
                                "this staff does not say how a realized span is spelled",
                            )?,
                            items,
                        )),
                    },
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // One region of staff notation, read into one ordinary expression.
    //
    // The region's own braces are what the traversal is started outside of, so
    // a region written any other way arrives here with nothing taken and is
    // told what a region is written between.
    let expand = fn (region) {
        match recurse_syntax(
            fn (state, here) { missing_read(region, state, here) },
            fn (state, here, kind, text) { token_read(region, state, here, kind, text) },
            fn (state, here, name) {
                token_read(region, state, here, TokenKind.Identifier, name)
            },
            fn (state, here, delimiter, kids) {
                group_read(region, state, here, delimiter, kids)
            },
            Unopened,
            region,
        ) {
            Unopened -> Err((region, "a staff region is written between braces")),
            Opened(place, pending) -> match pending {
                Pending(read, length, dots, tying, numbers, taken, voiced, words) -> match taken {
                    NoBody -> Err((region, "a staff region is written between braces")),
                    Took(spot, body) -> document_read(region, spot, body),
                },
            },
        }
    };

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- writing

    // The other direction, and the one that makes this adapter *generative*.
    // Everything below is the printer's: `expand` names none of it, so none of
    // it is checked in the expansion phase, which is what lets it name the
    // package's own types. A reader and a writer for the same notation belong
    // in one module even though they are checked in two readings.

    // A word, then its parentheses if it takes numbers. `bar (4, 4)`,
    // `tuplet (3, 2)` and `time (4, 4)` are one shape, so they are one
    // function.
    fn counted(word: Text, count: Nat, unit: Nat) -> Text {
        text_join([word, " (", nat_literal(count), ", ", nat_literal(unit), ")"])
    }

    fn measured(word: Text, beats: Meter) -> Text {
        match beats {
            Beats(count, unit) -> counted(word, count, unit),
        }
    }

    // Two pieces of notation with a space between them, and no space where
    // either of them is nothing. An empty body is what `NoItems` writes, and a
    // page that ended with a stray space would not be this page reprinted.
    fn spaced(first: Text, second: Text) -> Text {
        if text_equal(first, "") {
            second
        } else if text_equal(second, "") {
            first
        } else { text_join([first, " ", second]) }
    }

    // A form that holds notation: its head, its braces, and whatever follows.
    fn wrapped(
        head: Text,
        body: Result<Text, Text>,
        after: Result<Text, Text>,
    ) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(spaced(text_join([head, " { ", body?, " }"]), after?)) }

    // The dots after a written value, one per dot.
    //
    // `nat_fold` and not a comparison, because the language has no `nat_equal`
    // and needs none: a count is eliminated by counting down it, which is what
    // a fold over a `Nat` is.
    fn dotted(dots: Nat) -> Text {
        nat_fold(
            "",
            fn (step_index: Nat, sofar: Text) -> Text { text_join([sofar, "."]) },
            dots,
        )
    }

    // What is written after a pitch: `/4`, `/4.`, or `(3/8)`.
    //
    // The first loss this printer can state. An exact span is a `Ratio` and the
    // grammar has no literal for one below zero, so `ratio_literal` answers
    // nothing and the answer here is a sentence rather than a shorter note.
    fn written_value(held: WrittenDuration) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match held {
            NoteValue(division, dots) -> Ok(text_join([
                "/",
                nat_literal(division),
                dotted(dots),
            ])),
            ExactSpan(span) -> option_fold(
                Err("this staff writes no exact span below zero, and this note holds one"),
                fn (spelt: Text) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(text_join(["(", spelt, ")"])) },
                ratio_literal(span),
            ),
        }
    }

    fn tied_on(tied: Tie) -> Text { match tied { Untied -> "", TiedOn -> " ~", } }

    // The pitches of a chord or a grace, in the order they are held.
    fn voiced_pitches(voices: List<Pitch>) -> Text {
        list_fold_from_start(
            "",
            fn (sung: Pitch, sofar: Text) -> Text { spaced(sofar, pitch_literal(sung)) },
            voices,
        )
    }

    // What is written at one point of the staff.
    fn written_event(event: StaffEvent) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match event {
            Note(spelled, held, tied) -> Ok(text_join([
                pitch_literal(spelled),
                written_value(held)?,
                tied_on(tied),
            ])),
            Rest(silent) -> Ok(text_join(["rest", written_value(silent)?])),
            Chord(voices, sustained, joined) -> Ok(text_join([
                "[",
                voiced_pitches(voices),
                "]",
                written_value(sustained)?,
                tied_on(joined),
            ])),
            Grace(ornaments) -> Ok(text_join(["grace [", voiced_pitches(ornaments), "]"])),
            MeterChange(beats) -> Ok(measured("meter", beats)),
        }
    }

    // The written sequence, by the fold the package's own `data` generates.
    //
    // A `pickup` is printed as the `bar` it is. The package has no pickup —
    // a pickup is a bar with fewer beats in it — so there is nothing here to
    // tell the two apart, and the round-trip law is stated on the value for
    // exactly this reason.
    fn written_items(items: StaffItem) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        staff_item_fold(
            Ok(""),
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                event: StaffEvent,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(spaced(written_event(event)?, after?)) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                beats: Meter,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped(measured("bar", beats), body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped("slur", body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                played: Nat,
                against: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped(counted("tuplet", played, against), body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                times: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                wrapped(text_join(["repeat (", nat_literal(times), ")"]), body, after)
            },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                pass: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                wrapped(text_join(["ending (", nat_literal(pass), ")"]), body, after)
            },
            items,
        )
    }

    fn clef_word(written: Clef) -> Text {
        match written {
            Treble -> "treble",
            Bass -> "bass",
            Alto -> "alto",
            Tenor -> "tenor",
        }
    }

    fn spelling_word(chosen: Spelling) -> Text {
        match chosen {
            ShortestReadable -> "shortest_readable",
            ExactValues -> "exact_values",
        }
    }

    // The second loss, and the one the trial note asked for. A transposing
    // instrument is written by naming the interval its part sits above what it
    // sounds, and the written names cover a grid: an interval outside it, and
    // any descending one, has no name a reader would take. The document is not
    // made smaller by leaving it out — the loss says which part could not be
    // written.
    fn transposing_word(shift: Interval) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        option_fold(
            Err("this staff has no written name for the interval this part is transposed by"),
            fn (spelt: Text) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(text_join(["transposing ", spelt])) },
            interval_literal(shift),
        )
    }

    // One command, `replace`: put this text where the node the command names
    // stands.
    //
    // The node is named by anchor and the anchor is echoed straight back, which
    // is the whole of what an adapter may do with a place. The compiler owns
    // the translation to a range, and this adapter has no operation for reading
    // one. Everything the edit law asks for follows: the patch lands inside the
    // region because an anchor of this region is inside it, nothing outside the
    // named range is touched because nothing else is returned, and re-expanding
    // the patched region agrees with replacing that node because replacing it
    // is what the patch does.
    //
    // Every item this adapter emits carries an anchor, so `replace` reaches any
    // note, bar, tuplet, slur, repeat or ending on the page — which is the edit
    // §2.6 of the trial note draws, and more besides.
    let edit = fn (region, command, anchor, argument) {
        match command {
            "replace" -> Ok([(anchor, argument)]),
            _ -> Err("`staff` serves one command, `replace`"),
        }
    };

    // A page, written back as the notation it was read from.
    //
    // The header in the order a musician states it, then the notation. What is
    // written here is a region's *contents* — what goes between the braces of
    // `syntax staff { … }` — because the region's name is the importing file's
    // word for this package and not this package's word for itself.
    let print = fn (document: StaffDocument) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match document {
            Document(instrument, sounding_shift, written_clef, written_key, beats, spelling, items,) -> Ok(text_join(
                [
                    "instrument \"",
                    instrument,
                    "\" ",
                    transposing_word(sounding_shift)?,
                    " clef ",
                    clef_word(written_clef),
                    " ",
                    key_literal(written_key),
                    " ",
                    measured("time", beats),
                    " spelling ",
                    spelling_word(spelling),
                    " ",
                    written_items(items)?,
                ],
            )),
        }
    };
}
